{"title":"Is the whole more forecastable than its parts? Testing the aggregation ladder","for":"Luca","claim":"Single stocks are a three-body problem, but an aggregate (the market, a fund, a group) should be more forecastable than the stocks in it, the way temperature is forecastable and molecules aren't.","claim_source":"Luca, two messages, 2026-10-23 (claim A of experiments/untested-luca-market-geometry.md)","version_tested":"Every month from 2003 (scored 2008-01 to 2019-12, 144 months), the 500 most-traded US stocks are rebuilt into a ladder: single stocks; 1,000 random equal-weight baskets each of 5, 25 and 100 stocks; and the equal-weight market of about 1,290 stocks. Three plain price signals (12-month trend skipping the last month, minus the last 5-day return, the last 5- or 21-day return) forecast the next 5 or 21 days after a skipped day, each turned into a forecast by a straight line fitted only on earlier months. Forecastability is out-of-sample R² against each level's own historical average, so a rising market can't pass for skill. A1: does R² rise with ln(basket size)? Pass needs a bootstrap p below 0.0013 and the market itself forecastable (Clark-West t >= 3). A2: do co-moving groups (stocks sharing a fund) beat random baskets matched on beta and volatility? Control: the same ladder for a volatility forecast.","verdict":{"short":"A1 fails; A2 is not passed; the volatility control also failed the slope bar.","detail":"No rung of the ladder was forecastable in direction: 24 of 25 R² values are at or below zero, and R² falls, not rises, from single stocks to 100-stock baskets. The best slope (+0.08 R² points per unit of ln size, AR(1) at 21 days) has p = 0.38 against a bar of 0.0013, and the market's best Clark-West t is 0.71 against 3. Groups lose less than matched baskets for all five forecasters (best p 0.017, 0.084 after correcting for five), but neither beats its average, and the same gap appears with no real relation at all. Volatility is forecastable at every level (market Clark-West t 3.2), yet its slope p is 0.25, so the ladder as specified has little power; what it does show is that direction R² is negative, not small and positive.","a1_raw_one_sided_p":0.38246175,"a2_raw_one_sided_p":0.01679832},"key_numbers":[{"label":"Best slope of forecastability on basket size","value":"+0.08 R² points per ln-size unit (p = 0.38)","meaning":"Going from one stock to the whole market added about half a point of R² in the best case, well within chance; the bar was p < 0.0013."},{"label":"Forecaster-level combinations beating their own average","value":"1 of 25","meaning":"Against each level's historical mean, the five forecasters lost on every rung but one (the market, AR(1) at 21 days, +0.55%, not significant)."},{"label":"The market's best Clark-West t","value":"0.71 (bar: 3)","meaning":"The market itself was not forecastable in direction by any of the five forecasters."},{"label":"Volatility control: market Clark-West t / slope p","value":"3.2 / 0.25","meaning":"Volatility is forecastable at every level, but even it did not pass the ladder's slope bar: the test as written is weak."},{"label":"Groups minus matched baskets (trend, 21 days)","value":"+1.31 R² points (p 0.017; 0.084 corrected)","meaning":"Industry groups lose less than look-alike random baskets, but both are below zero, and with every real relation destroyed the gap is already +0.55 (sd 0.49): mostly a fitting effect."},{"label":"SPY R², against zero vs against its average","value":"+4.2% vs +0.13% (AR(1), 21 days)","meaning":"Against zero, SPY looks forecastable only because it drifts up; against its own average it is not."}],"charts":[{"id":"ladder","title":"Forecastability by basket size (2008–2019)","x_label":"Stocks in the basket (log scale)","y_label":"Out-of-sample R² against the historical mean (%)","note":"Points: the five ladder levels with 90% bootstrap intervals. null_r2_pct: what the same fit scores when every real relation is destroyed (sign-flip null); it slopes down by itself because basket signals are nearly identical within a month. other_levels are reported, not in the slope; group sizes average 12.","return_forecasters":[{"forecaster":"Trend, 5 days","key":"trend_h5","points":[{"level":"Single stocks","key":"stock","size":1.0,"r2_pct":-0.1727,"ci90_pct":[-0.3719,0.0257]},{"level":"Random baskets of 5","key":"b5","size":5.0,"r2_pct":-0.552,"ci90_pct":[-1.0034,-0.1277]},{"level":"Random baskets of 25","key":"b25","size":25.0,"r2_pct":-1.1893,"ci90_pct":[-2.3516,-0.119]},{"level":"Random baskets of 100","key":"b100","size":100.0,"r2_pct":-1.4892,"ci90_pct":[-3.0139,-0.086]},{"level":"The market (equal-weight, ~1,290 stocks)","key":"market","size":1289.6,"r2_pct":-3.3522,"ci90_pct":[-7.4059,0.2266]}],"other_levels":[{"level":"Co-moving groups (share a twin fund)","key":"group","size":12.2,"r2_pct":-0.9045,"ci90_pct":[-1.8505,-0.0911]},{"level":"Matched random baskets","key":"matched","size":12.2,"r2_pct":-1.322,"ci90_pct":[-3.3734,0.2848]},{"level":"The groups' twin funds","key":"fund","size":12.2,"r2_pct":-1.1368,"ci90_pct":[-2.61,0.0594]},{"level":"SPY","key":"spy","size":500.0,"r2_pct":-1.3118,"ci90_pct":[-2.2198,-0.4846]}],"null_r2_pct":{"stock":-0.1528,"b5":-0.5141,"b25":-0.9662,"b100":-1.1767,"market":-1.4286},"slope":-0.4333,"slope_ci90":[-0.9872,0.0595],"slope_boot_p":0.9189,"market_clark_west_t":-1.09},{"forecaster":"Trend, 21 days","key":"trend_h21","points":[{"level":"Single stocks","key":"stock","size":1.0,"r2_pct":-0.1641,"ci90_pct":[-0.4784,0.0773]},{"level":"Random baskets of 5","key":"b5","size":5.0,"r2_pct":-0.7732,"ci90_pct":[-1.4485,-0.2163]},{"level":"Random baskets of 25","key":"b25","size":25.0,"r2_pct":-1.629,"ci90_pct":[-2.8296,-0.545]},{"level":"Random baskets of 100","key":"b100","size":100.0,"r2_pct":-1.981,"ci90_pct":[-3.5384,-0.6126]},{"level":"The market (equal-weight, ~1,290 stocks)","key":"market","size":1289.6,"r2_pct":-2.2015,"ci90_pct":[-4.5941,-0.3227]}],"other_lev